WEBVTT

00:00.457 --> 00:01.972
- [Instructor] Pamela was asked to find

00:01.972 --> 00:03.856
where h of x equals x to the third

00:03.856 --> 00:08.270
minus six x squared plus 12
x has a relative extremum.

00:08.270 --> 00:09.784
This is her solution.

00:09.784 --> 00:12.528
So step one, it looks like she
tried to take the derivative.

00:12.528 --> 00:15.102
Step two, she tries to find the solution,

00:15.102 --> 00:17.026
find where the derivative is equal to zero

00:17.026 --> 00:19.075
and she found that it
happens at x equals two

00:19.075 --> 00:21.240
so she says that's a critical point.

00:21.240 --> 00:23.692
In step three, she says,
she makes the conclusion

00:23.692 --> 00:27.372
that therefore h has a
relative extremum there.

00:27.372 --> 00:29.460
Is Pamela's work correct?

00:29.460 --> 00:31.625
If not, what's her mistake?

00:31.625 --> 00:33.749
So pause this video and try
to work through it yourself

00:33.749 --> 00:36.240
and see if Pamela's work is correct.

00:36.240 --> 00:37.261
Alright, well, I'm just gonna try

00:37.261 --> 00:38.669
to do it again in parallel.

00:38.669 --> 00:40.293
So first, let me just
take the derivative here.

00:40.293 --> 00:42.167
So h prime of x.

00:42.167 --> 00:43.599
Just use the power rule multiple times.

00:43.599 --> 00:48.010
It's gonna be three x squared
for the x to the third.

00:48.010 --> 00:52.342
Two times negative six is
negative 12 or minus 12 x

00:52.342 --> 00:55.701
and then the derivative of 12
x is plus 12 and let's see.

00:55.701 --> 00:57.975
You can factor out a three here.

00:57.975 --> 01:02.605
So it's three times x squared
minus four x plus four

01:02.605 --> 01:06.168
and this part is indeed
equal to x minus two squared.

01:06.168 --> 01:10.743
So this is equal to three
times x minus two squared.

01:10.743 --> 01:14.098
So her step one looks right on target.

01:14.098 --> 01:17.099
Okay, step two, the
solution of h prime of x

01:17.099 --> 01:20.272
is equal to zero is equal to x equals two.

01:20.272 --> 01:21.581
Yeah, that works out.

01:21.581 --> 01:22.848
If you were to say,

01:22.848 --> 01:26.244
if you were to say three
times x minus two squared

01:26.244 --> 01:29.075
which is h prime of x,
the first derivative,

01:29.075 --> 01:30.421
and set that equal to zero,

01:30.421 --> 01:34.103
this is going to be true
when x is equal to two

01:34.103 --> 01:36.921
and so any point where
your first derivative

01:36.921 --> 01:39.861
is equal to zero or it's undefined,

01:39.861 --> 01:42.231
it is indeed a critical point.

01:42.231 --> 01:45.217
So this step looks good so far

01:45.217 --> 01:49.308
and then step three, h
has a relative extremum

01:49.308 --> 01:50.891
at x equals two.

01:51.764 --> 01:53.690
Alright, so she made
a big conclusion here.

01:53.690 --> 01:56.918
She assumed that because
the derivative was zero,

01:56.918 --> 01:59.164
that we have a relative extremum.

01:59.164 --> 02:01.908
So let's just see if you can
even just make that conclusion.

02:01.908 --> 02:04.653
In order to have a relative extremum,

02:04.653 --> 02:07.142
your curve is gonna
look something like this

02:07.142 --> 02:09.721
and then you would have a
relative extremum right over here

02:09.721 --> 02:12.753
and over here, your slope
goes from being positive

02:12.753 --> 02:16.594
and then it hits zero and
then it goes to being negative

02:16.594 --> 02:19.172
or you could have a
relative extremum like this.

02:19.172 --> 02:21.665
This would be a maximum point.

02:21.665 --> 02:25.415
This would be a minimum
point right over here

02:26.301 --> 02:28.106
and then in a minimum point,

02:28.106 --> 02:29.578
your slope is zero right over there

02:29.578 --> 02:31.464
but right before it,
your slope was negative

02:31.464 --> 02:33.797
and it goes to being positive

02:33.797 --> 02:36.501
but you actually have cases
where your derivative,

02:36.501 --> 02:38.182
your first derivative is zero

02:38.182 --> 02:40.717
but you don't have an extremum.

02:40.717 --> 02:45.419
So for example, you could
have a point like this

02:45.419 --> 02:47.252
where right over here,

02:48.203 --> 02:52.495
your slope or your derivative
could be equal to zero

02:52.495 --> 02:54.094
and so your first derivative
would be equal to zero

02:54.094 --> 02:56.302
but notice, your slope
is positive, it hits zero

02:56.302 --> 02:59.250
and then it goes back
to being positive again

02:59.250 --> 03:01.461
and so you can't make the conclusion

03:01.461 --> 03:03.908
just because your derivative is zero

03:03.908 --> 03:05.665
that it's definitely an extremum.

03:05.665 --> 03:06.732
You could say it's a critical point

03:06.732 --> 03:08.243
and so step two is correct.

03:08.243 --> 03:10.204
In order to make this conclusion,

03:10.204 --> 03:12.624
you would have to test what
the derivative is doing

03:12.624 --> 03:14.862
before that point and after that point

03:14.862 --> 03:17.599
and verify that it is switching sides

03:17.599 --> 03:19.028
and we could try to do that.

03:19.028 --> 03:22.483
So let's make a little table here.

03:22.483 --> 03:24.347
Make a little table.

03:24.347 --> 03:25.606
Do it a little bit neater.

03:25.606 --> 03:29.023
So x, x, h prime of x right over here.

03:31.172 --> 03:34.799
We know at x equals two,
h prime of two is zero.

03:34.799 --> 03:36.440
That's our critical point.

03:36.440 --> 03:37.911
But let's try, I don't know,

03:37.911 --> 03:40.902
let's see what happens
when x is equal to one

03:40.902 --> 03:42.618
and then let's see what
happens when x equals three.

03:42.618 --> 03:45.371
I'm just sampling points
on either side of two

03:45.371 --> 03:47.907
and let's see, we are going to have,

03:47.907 --> 03:48.970
when x is equal to one,

03:48.970 --> 03:53.720
h prime of one is three
times one minus two squared.

03:53.720 --> 03:55.763
One minus two, negative
one squared is positive one

03:55.763 --> 03:59.688
times three is still
positive and then three.

03:59.688 --> 04:02.061
Well, three minus two squared times three.

04:02.061 --> 04:03.626
That's also gonna be three.

04:03.626 --> 04:05.478
So this is actually a situation where,

04:05.478 --> 04:07.743
like I've just drawn it
where our slope is positive

04:07.743 --> 04:09.359
before we hit the critical point,

04:09.359 --> 04:13.448
it gets to zero but then it
starts becoming positive again

04:13.448 --> 04:15.985
and so that's why you
actually have to do this test

04:15.985 --> 04:18.108
in order to identify
whether it's an extremum.

04:18.108 --> 04:19.863
It turns out that this is not an extremum.

04:19.863 --> 04:23.130
This is not a maximum
or a minimum point here.

04:23.130 --> 04:25.421
So Pamela's work is not correct

04:25.421 --> 04:27.553
and her mistake is step three.

04:27.553 --> 04:28.901
In order to make this conclusion,

04:28.901 --> 04:30.697
you would have to test on either side

04:30.697 --> 04:31.672
of that critical point.

04:31.672 --> 04:32.505
Test the first derivative.
